¶
'''alectryomachy''' (a-lek-tri-om′a-ki), ''n.'' [< Gr. ἀλεκτρυών, a cock, + μάχη, a fight.] Cock-fighting. Sometimes written ''alectoromachy''.

'''alectryomancy''' (a-lek′tri-ō-man″si), ''n.'' [< F. ''alectryomantie'' (Cotgrave), < Gr. ἀλεκτρυών, a cock, + μαντεία, divination.] An ancient practice of foretelling events by means of a cock.
{{smaller block|The letters of the alphabet were traced on the ground in squares within a circle, and a grain of corn was placed on each; a cock was then permitted to pick up the grains, and the letters under them, being formed into words in the order of their selection by the cock, were supposed to foretell the event. Sometimes written ''alectoromancy''.}}

'''Alectryon''' (a-lek′tri-ọn), ''n.'' [NL., < Gr. ἀλεκτρυών, a cock: see ''Alector''.] '''1.''' In ''ornith.'', a genus of birds, proposed by Cabanis in 1846 for a section of the Macartney pheasants, genus ''Euplocamus'' of Temminck. The type is ''A. erythrophthalmus'' of Malacca.—'''2.''' A poetical name of the domestic cock.
{{smaller block|Loud the cock ''Alectryon'' crowed.
{{right|''Longfellow.''}}}}

'''ale-draper†''' (āl′drā″pėr), ''n.'' [< ''ale'' + ''draper'', as in ''linen-draper'': a humorous name, perhaps in allusion to the old ale-yard: see ''ale-yard''.] An ale-house keeper.
{{smaller block|I get mee a wife; with her a little money; when we are married, seeke a house we must; no other occupation have I but to be an ''ale-draper''.
{{right|''Henry Chettle'', Kind-Hart’s Dreame (1592).}}
So that nowe hee hath lefte brokery, and is become a draper. A draper, quoth Freeman, what draper, of woollin or linnen? No, qd [quod, quoth] he, an ''ale-draper'', wherein he hath more skil then [than] in the other.
{{right|''Discoverie of Knights of the Poste'', 1597. (''Halliwell.'')}}}}

